after russia annexed crimea in 2014 and  faced sanctions imposed by the west  russian president vladimir putin turned  to china and his friend president xi  jinping  [Music]  that may the two countries signed a 400  billion gas supply agreement since then  cooperation between putin and ji has  only strengthened and back in february  the two leaders declared that their  friendship had no limits but now  russia's violent invasion of ukraine is  testing the strength of that partnership  and with fears that beijing could help  moscow as it continues its military  assault on ukraine that partnership is  being watched closely around the world  china will bear responsibility for any  actions it takes to support russia's  aggression the cost of this war for  china  could potentially be very high i spoke  to my colleague ling ling wei wsj's  chief china correspondent about three  ways that the war in ukraine is weighing  on china  first there's china's economy which is  important not just to the chinese  population but to president xi  personally since he's seeking to extend  his rule for an unprecedented third term  this fall for him stability economic  stability political stability social  stability really becomes everything else  so the ukraine crisis really made his  existing domestic economic challenge  much harder not only are china's  businesses and manufacturers being hit  by soaring commodity prices and supply  chain breakdowns sparked by the war  they're scrambling to assess the risk of  becoming collateral damage when it comes  to the west's sanctions on russia they  do not want to lose access to markets in  the developed world markets are way more  important  to china than the russian market plus  for china this economic turmoil being  brought on by the war is coming on top  of economic pain that was already caused  by the pandemic and president g's  crackdown on capitalist forces something  he's rolled back recently the war is  also threatening china's global image  and its efforts to be seen as a peaceful  and benevolent superpower beijing has  denied knowing about putin's invasion  plans in advance and it has repeatedly  called for dialogue to resolve the  crisis but just weeks before the  invasion began putin and g held a summit  and released a 5 000 word statement  outlining a vision for a new world order  that caused many in the west to label  china and russia a new axis by tilting  toward russia  on the eve of the invasion  china has basically painted itself as an  enabler of sort of a country that is now  being universally condemned ling ling  says that contradicts china's efforts to  uphold itself as a responsible  stakeholder in global affairs for  example china has promoted itself as a  well-intentioned world power by  investing in infrastructure projects in  other countries offering kovid 19  vaccines to the developing world and  stepping up efforts to work with  international organizations like the un  so why is there even a question of  whether china would help or even  remain close to russia at this point  that's a great question xi jinping  definitely has an interest in keeping  russia around as an ally both are  authoritarians and nationalists with the  desire to really raise their country's  statue and challenge the us-led world  order so far china has declined to  criticize russia or describe its actions  in ukraine as an invasion chinese  officials have blamed the us for  provoking russia and third russia's  invasion of ukraine has put a fresh  spotlight on china's intentions when it  comes to taiwan which beijing sees as  part of china the u.s has strengthened  ties with the self-governed democratic  island in recent years i think the scale  of the u.s and its allied response to  the war and the scale of sanctions have  really  surprised many people in china china  hasn't given an indication that it plans  to take action on taiwan in the near  future but it has raised tensions by  flying fighter jets near the island and  ramping up the military pressure western  officials worry that with putin arguing  that ukraine is historically part of  russia china could be encouraged to make  a similar argument to try to bring  taiwan into its fold as russia's war in  ukraine grinds on and as china continues  to face pressure in responding to it we  will have all of the updates at wsj.com  and if you thought this video was  informative hit the like button and  subscribe to the channel for more  stories like this i'll see you next time  [Music]
 

